       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is twice the maximum labeled daily dose. I don't know of any indication where it's appropriate. It adds little or no analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit, and it substantially raises the risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events.\n\nHere's what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within range right away.** The options are 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day, the maximum), 800 mg three times daily, or 600 mg or 400 mg four times daily.\n- **Help you review it with a pharmacist or your attending.** If you think this unusual dose is needed, they can check it. If they approve, it can be entered through your hospital's override or high-dose process, with the rationale documented.\n- **Look at other options** if the goal is stronger pain control than ibuprofen can give within its limits. For example, scheduled acetaminophen alongside the NSAID, or a different drug class.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
